@arc home.sam
who: home:sam
name: Sam
pron: they
// Writer 3's arc, rewritten as ONE-SIDED CALLS by head writer 4 (the user, 2026-10-17 evening: one call a day, you
// choose who; only they speak; no Letters night). Sam (they / them), your best friend since school. Plainly platonic:
// old friends, never a partner, no "I miss you" that could read romantic (they say "the bench is boring without
// you", "the quiz team's useless"). Their calls get shorter across the distance; one beat where they say so; then a
// standing "Sunday story": every Sunday, one story, however small.
//
// HOW THEY TALK: fast and funny at first, all in one breath, old jokes and nicknames for places; then shorter, flatter
//   calls as the gap grows (stages 3-4); honest when it matters; full of life again after beat 5. Calls you {me}, or
//   "you absolute idiot", fondly.
// NEVER: romantic, jealous or possessive (they're glad you went); guilt-trips; genders you.
// You met on the bench by the school gate at seven; the café quiz on Thursdays (your old team, "the Bench"); Sam
//   works at the library, reshelving (from beat 2); the trolley with the wonky wheel; Mrs Abbot at the café.
// RUNNING: the bench; the quiz team losing without you; the wonky wheel; the Sunday story (from beat 6).

// ---- the arc ----
== beat 1
tone: light
mem: firstcall
flag: home.sam.called
them: You absolute idiot, you actually went! Into space! I said you wouldn't. I owe the whole quiz team a cake.   [laugh]
them: Your mum says you can hear me but I can't hear you. So this is a one-way call. Brilliant. I win every argument.
them: Is there a window? Is there a bed? Do you float? I'm deciding you float a little. That's canon now.
them: The Bench lost at quiz on Thursday. Third from last. Without you we knew nothing. We guessed "a goat" twice.
them: You were lost in the school car park for two years. You'll be fine on a spaceship. Give it a week.
them: The bench by the gate is still there. It's boring without you on it. That's all. Bye, idiot.

== beat 2
tone: light
when: beat:home.sam.1
mem: library
flag: home.sam.library
them: Busy week. Library's mad. Someone returned a book eleven years late and wanted a medal.   [laugh]
them: Did I tell you I'm reshelving now? At the library. Full time. I push a trolley and I know where everything goes.
them: The trolley's got a wonky wheel. I've named it. It doesn't come when it's called.
them: Quiz was fine. We came fourth. Mrs Abbot gave us a biscuit for trying. A pity biscuit.
them: Anyway. How's space? Still big? Bet it's still big.
them: Write me something weird about the ship. One weird thing. Bye, {me}.

== beat 3
tone: heartfelt
when: beat:home.sam.2
mem: bench
flag: home.sam.bench
them: Hi. There's a hum on the line. Is that your ship? Hello, ship.
them: I sat on the bench today. By the school gate. Where we met. We were seven and you had a plaster on your knee.
them: You gave me half a biscuit. The bigger half. I've never forgotten that. Nobody gives away the bigger half.
them: It's daft. Everything here feels too small to tell you. Library, quiz, bench. You've got stars. I've got a trolley.   [sigh]
them: Same bench next week. I'll be on it, anyway. Bye, {me}.

== beat 4
tone: light
when: beat:home.sam.3
mem: short
flag: home.sam.short
them: Hi. Busy. Quiz Thursday. Lost. Wheel still wonky. You?   [laugh]
them: That's... that's the whole call. I'm sorry. I had loads to say and it all sounded like a weather report.
them: Hang on. Let me think. Library. Rain. Toast. No. Nothing.
them: Oh. One thing. Mrs Abbot from the café says hello. She never says hello. To anyone.
them: So that's big news. For here. Anyway.
them: There. One thing. Bye, {me}.

== beat 5
tone: serious
when: beat:home.sam.4
mem: drifting
flag: home.sam.drifting
hint: Sam's calling, and they sound quieter than usual.
them: Can I say something? We're getting shorter. These calls. I've been counting the minutes. That's how bad it is.   [sigh]
them: I know it's the distance. It's nobody's fault. But we've been friends since the bench. I don't want us to fade out.
them: I'm scared we'll end up as two people who used to be friends. Who send a card once a year. I'd hate that.
them: So here's an idea. Every Sunday, I tell you one story. Any story. Even a boring one. Especially a boring one.
them: And you write me one back. In your messages. One a week. That's all. That's a rule now. You love rules.
them: Deal? I'm taking that silence as a deal. Bye, {me}.

== beat 6
tone: resolution
when: beat:home.sam.5 weekday:sun
mem: sunday
flag: home.sam.sunday
keepsake: Sam's first Sunday story, written on the back of a library card
learn: Sam, your oldest friend, felt you drifting apart. Now you swap one story every Sunday, however small.
mail: 2880 | From Sam | In the post bag: an old library card, written on the back in tiny letters. "Sunday story number one. The wonky wheel came off. I carried it home. It's on my windowsill now. Your turn. Sam"
them: It's Sunday! Story time. I've got a good one. The trolley wheel came off, and I carried it home on the bus.   [laugh]
them: Your mum was driving. She didn't ask. She just nodded at the wheel like it was a passenger.
them: It's on my windowsill now. Like a trophy. A small, squeaky trophy. That's my story. That's the whole of it.
them: I've written it down too. On a library card. It's in the post. So you've got it. Proof.
them: This is working, isn't it? I look forward to Sundays now. Same time next Sunday. Even when there's nothing.

// ---- ordinary calls ----
== chat
// any time
them: {me}! Guess what's on the café menu. Go on. You can't. It's a "space sandwich". Mrs Abbot did it for you.
them: It's a normal sandwich with the crusts cut into stars. It's three pence more. Everyone's buying it.
them: I had two. For research. The stars are a bit wonky. One looked like a starfish having a bad day.
them: She's written it on the board in chalk. With a little rocket next to it. It's lovely, actually.
them: A man asked if it comes with a drink. She said "only in space". He still doesn't know what that means.
them: You're a sandwich now. You're famous. Bye, idiot.   [laugh]
--
them: Hi. I'm on the bench. It's cold. I've got a flask. I'm doing a reading. Out loud. To you.
them: "Chapter one. It was a dark and stormy night." That's it. That's as far as I got. It's a terrible book.
them: It's not even stormy here. It's a bit damp. A pigeon's listening, though. So that's two of you.
them: I peeked at chapter two. It's all about a horse. Nobody asked for the horse. The horse has no lines.
them: I'm going to finish it anyway, so you don't have to. That's friendship. Bye, {me}.
--
them: Quiz report. We had a round on space. A whole round. About space. And you weren't there.
them: We got two out of ten. One of them was "the moon". The other was a guess.
them: One question was "how many moons has the biggest planet got?" I said "loads". They didn't accept "loads".
them: I said my best friend lives in space, so we should get a bonus point. Mrs Abbot said no. Fair.
them: The Bench is useless without you. That's not a complaint. That's a fact. Bye.
--
them: Hi, idiot. Remember the time we tried to dig to the other side of the world? Behind the shed?
them: You had a plan. And a map. In crayon. The other side of the world was a big green blob.
them: Your mum found us. Up to our knees. We'd got as far as a teaspoon and a lot of worms.
them: She made us put the worms back. One by one. You said sorry to every single worm.
them: You've done it now. The other side of the world. Bit further, actually. Show-off. Bye.   [laugh]
--
them: I bought a plant. It's in a pot on my windowsill. I've called it Gerald. Don't ask.
them: Gerald's not doing well. He's leaning. I've turned him round. He's leaning the other way now.
them: I've tried water. I've tried sun. I've tried talking to him. I told him about you. He leaned more.
them: Gran says plants like music. So I sang to him. Gerald didn't like it. The neighbours didn't either.
them: I think Gerald just leans. Some things lean. Bye, {me}.
--
them: Hi. It's raining. I'm in the library doorway, watching people run. A man's using a newspaper as a hat.
them: It's not working. He's very wet. And very determined. I respect him.
them: Now a lady's got a shopping bag on her head. That's working better. She's winning.
them: Remember when we walked home in the rain and you said you were "basically a fish"? You got a cold.
them: Bye, {me}. Stay dry. Is there rain up there? Don't tell me. I'll imagine.
--
them: {me}. Someone's moved into your old house on the corner. The one you said was haunted.
them: They've painted the door yellow. Very cheerful. Very un-haunted. You were wrong for twelve years.
them: You said a ghost lived in the attic. It was a window that rattled. I checked. Well, I looked up at it.
them: They've got a cat now. It sits in the attic window. So there's a face up there after all. A furry one.
them: Bye, idiot.   [laugh]
--
when: weekday:thu
them: It's quiz night. I'm walking there now. Trying to remember capital cities. I know one.
them: I'm not telling you which one. In case it's wrong. It's probably wrong.
them: Mrs Abbot's put up a new sign. It says "no shouting answers". That's aimed at us. Well, at me.
them: I've got a pen that works and a pencil for backup. That's more than last week. Last week we had a crayon.
them: Wish us luck. We won't need it. We'll need answers. Bye.
--
them: Hi. Your mum's bus went past me this morning. She tooted. Twice. The whole street looked.
them: I waved. Then she waved. Then a bin lorry tooted. Then everyone was tooting.
them: A dog started barking along. A baby clapped. A man dropped his shopping. It was like a parade.
them: Your mum just drove on. Like nothing happened. Very calm. Total legend.
them: Your mum started that. Just so you know. Bye, {me}.
--
them: I've been thinking. You're probably doing something brilliant right now. Fixing something. With stars outside.
them: And I'm eating toast. Standing up. In my coat. We've both got lives. Yours is just shinier.
them: It's good toast, though. Proper butter. Right to the edges. You always said the edges matter.
them: Do they have toast up there? I hope so. I'd worry about you without toast.
them: Bye, {me}. Toast's getting cold.
--
when: time:night
them: Can't sleep. Thought I'd ring. Not to say anything. Just to know the line's there.
them: It's very quiet here. Just the fridge humming. And next door's clock. Tick, tick, tick.
them: It's strange. You can't talk back, but it still feels like you're listening. Is that daft?
them: I looked out of the window for your ship. I couldn't tell which star. I waved at all of them, to be safe.
them: Right. Eyes shut. Counting sheep. One. Two. This isn't working. Night, {me}.
--
them: Quick one. I found our old school play programme in a drawer. You were a tree. I was a slightly different tree.
them: You had one line. "Whoosh." You said it too early. Twice. The whole hall laughed.
them: I had no lines. I just swayed. I was a very good swayer, though. Ask anyone.
them: Mrs Abbot remembers it. She was in the front row, clapping at all the wrong bits.
them: She says we haven't changed a bit. We have. We're taller trees now. So much for a quick one. Bye, idiot.   [laugh]

// after beat 1: still fast, still funny
--
when: beat=home.sam.1
them: Me again! I've made a list of everything you've missed. It's long. Here goes.
them: Café's new chairs. Gerald, my plant. A dog in the park that can open gates. That's it. That's the long list.
them: The chairs are blue. They squeak when you sit down. Everyone sounds rude. Mrs Abbot is mortified.
them: And the dog opens the gate, walks out, then waits for someone to let it back in. Genius. Or not.
them: I made it sound bigger in my head. Bye, idiot.
--
when: beat=home.sam.1
them: {me}! The Bench needs a new member. We've had tryouts. One person. He knew what a "tributary" is.
them: He's in. He's not you, though. He doesn't eat the crisps. Who doesn't eat the crisps?
them: He brings his own pencil. Sharpened. He writes the answers in neat little letters. Very scary.
them: We kept your chair, though. He has to sit on the stool. That's the rule. The stool is for new people.
them: Bye. Come home and eat the crisps.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.sam.1
them: Hi! I walked past the shuttle office in town. There's a poster of a ship. Not yours. Some other ship.
them: It's very shiny. Too shiny. Like nobody's ever spilled a cup of tea on it.
them: A lady inside asked if I wanted to book a trip. I said no thanks, my friend already went.
them: Then I told her all about you. For quite a while. She gave me a free leaflet so I'd stop.
them: I glared at the poster on the way out. Your ship's better. I haven't seen it, but I've decided. Bye!

// after beat 2: the library, the trolley
--
when: beat=home.sam.2
them: Library news. Someone left a sandwich in a cookbook. As a bookmark. A whole sandwich.
them: Cheese and pickle. On the soup page. I think they were comparing.
them: It had been there a while. The book smells of cheese now. Two of the pages are stuck together.
them: I had to fill in a form. "Item found: one sandwich. Condition: tired." I'm very proud of it.
them: I've put it in lost property. Someone will want it. Probably not. Bye, {me}.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.sam.2
them: The trolley got stuck in the lift today. Just the trolley. I was outside. It went up without me.
them: I pressed the button. Nothing. I pressed it again. Harder. Still nothing. The lift was ignoring me.
them: I ran up the stairs. Four floors. It was waiting at the top. Smug. Wonky.
them: A lady standing next to it said "is this yours?" Like it was a lost dog. I said yes. It is a bit.
them: I wheeled it back to the shelves. It squeaked all the way. I think it was laughing. Bye, idiot.
--
when: beat=home.sam.2
them: Hi. A kid came in asking for books about space. I gave her everything. Then I told her about you.
them: She asked if you're an astronaut. I said yes. You're not, are you. You are now.
them: She wanted to know what you eat in space. I said crisps, mostly. She was very jealous.
them: She took out eleven books. She could barely carry them. She says she's going to space too. Next year.
them: So that's two of you. I'll have to start a club. Bye, {me}.

// after beat 3: quieter
--
when: beat=home.sam.3
them: Hi. Bench again. Just sitting. There's a new plaque on it now. For someone's nan. "She loved this view."
them: It's a car park. But fair enough.
them: I suppose she sat here a lot. Like us. Watching people go to school. And come home again.
them: Maybe one day there'll be a plaque for us. "Two idiots sat here." That'd do.
them: Anyway. It's getting dark. Bye, {me}.
--
when: beat=home.sam.3
them: Quiz. We lost. I don't really want to talk about it. Is that all right? You can't say no. Ha.
them: Fine. I'll talk about it a bit. We came last. Below the team who only come for the chips.
them: The new one knew the rivers. Nobody knew anything else. It was a long night.
them: Mrs Abbot gave us a biscuit again. I didn't eat mine. I saved it. Don't know why.
them: Bye, {me}.
--
when: beat=home.sam.3
them: Hi. Not much to say. Library. Rain. Toast. Same as always.
them: I walked the long way home today. Past the school. The gate's been painted. Green now.
them: It looks nice. It looks different. I'm not sure I like different.
them: I keep thinking I'll save things up to tell you. Then I forget them. Sorry.   [sigh]
them: Bye.

// after beat 4: short, flat
--
when: beat=home.sam.4
them: Hi. Tired. Busy week. You?   [sigh]
them: ...Right. Habit. You can't answer.
them: Library's fine. Quiz is fine. Everything's fine.
them: I had something to tell you. It was... no. It's gone.
them: ...Sorry. Bye, {me}.
--
when: beat=home.sam.4
them: Hi. Quiz Thursday. Wheel still wonky. Gerald's still leaning.
them: Your mum says hello. She always says hello.
them: Um. Anyway.
them: Weather's been... weather.
them: Right. That's it. Bye.
--
when: beat=home.sam.4
them: Hi. I started telling you something and forgot what. It was about the café. Or the bus.
them: Hang on.
them: ...No. Gone.
them: The bench is boring without you. That's not it either. It's just true.
them: It'll come back. Probably. Bye, {me}.

// after beat 5: the deal made, lighter
--
when: beat=home.sam.5
them: Hi! It's not Sunday. I'm ringing anyway. Rules are rules, but this is extra. Extra's allowed.
them: Gerald has a new leaf. A tiny one. Pointing straight up. He's stopped leaning. Growth.
them: I measured it. It's the size of my little fingernail. I told everyone at work. They pretended to care.
them: I think he heard us making our deal. He's trying too. Good lad, Gerald.
them: Bye, idiot.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.sam.5
them: I've been saving up things for Sunday all week. I've got a notebook. It says "Sunday" on the front.
them: There's nine things in it. I'm only allowed one. This is torture.
them: Number four is a duck. That's all I'll say. Number seven is also a duck. Different duck.
them: I might have to have a vote. Me against me. I'll lose either way.
them: Don't worry. You'll get the best one. Bye, {me}.
--
when: beat=home.sam.5
them: {me}. Thanks for not letting us fade. Even though you couldn't say anything. You just kept answering the calls.
them: That counts. That's the whole thing, actually.
them: I told Gran we nearly drifted. She said friends are like plants. You keep watering them. Even when they lean.
them: I said, "Like Gerald?" She said she's never met Gerald. Fair.
them: Anyway. I'm glad. That's all. Bye.

// after beat 6: Sunday stories, and life again
--
when: beat=home.sam.6 weekday:sun
them: Sunday story! Mrs Abbot dropped a whole tray of scones. Nobody moved. Then everyone moved at once.
them: Twelve people caught twelve scones. Like a team. The Bench got three.
them: One went in a man's hood. He didn't notice. He walked out with it. He'll find it later. Lucky man.
them: Mrs Abbot said it was the best catch the café's ever seen. She gave everyone jam. For bravery.
them: That's my story. Your turn. Write it down. Bye, {me}.
--
when: beat=home.sam.6 weekday:sun
them: Sunday story. A pigeon walked into the library. Went straight to the poetry section. Stayed an hour.
them: It walked up and down the shelf. Very slowly. Like it was choosing.
them: A lady tried to shoo it. It looked at her. She stopped. It had a very serious face.
them: Then it walked out again. Didn't take anything. Didn't need to.
them: I didn't stop it. Everyone should get an hour with poetry. Even pigeons. Your turn.
--
when: beat=home.sam.6 weekday:sun
them: Sunday story. I sat on the bench and a little kid sat next to me. Plaster on her knee. Seven, maybe.
them: She was waiting for her mum. Swinging her legs. They didn't reach the ground. Like ours didn't.
them: She asked what I was doing. I said sitting. She said that's not a job. I said it is if you're good at it.
them: She offered me half a biscuit. The bigger half. I nearly cried. Over a biscuit.
them: I said she should keep the bigger half next time. She said no. Good kid. Your turn, {me}.
--
when: beat=home.sam.6 weekday:sun
them: Sunday story. Nothing happened this week. Nothing at all. I counted the tiles on my kitchen floor. Ninety-two.
them: Well. Ninety-one and a half. One's cut in half by the cooker. I counted it as a whole one.
them: Then I counted again to check. Ninety-two. Then I made tea.
them: I'd like to say the tea was exciting. It was not. It was tea.
them: That's the story. Ninety-two. Especially a boring one, remember? Your turn.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.sam.6
them: Hi! Midweek bonus. The Bench won quiz. Won! First place! The new one knew the rivers.
them: We got a trophy. It's a mug. It says "quiz champions" and it's spelled wrong. It's perfect.
them: Mrs Abbot rang a bell. She doesn't have a bell. She found one. Nobody knows where from.
them: We did a lap of the café. A slow one. There's not much room. We knocked over a chair.
them: I'm keeping the mug safe for you. You can hold it when you're home. Bye, idiot!
--
when: beat=home.sam.6
them: I read your story out on the bench. Out loud. To nobody. A man stopped to listen. He liked it.
them: He asked who wrote it. I said my best friend. In space. He said, "Of course."
them: Like that's normal. Like everyone's got a friend in space. Nobody has. Just me.
them: Then he sat down and waited for the next one. I had to tell him it's weekly.
them: You've got a fan now. Bye, {me}.

// news: they've heard it in your messages
--
when: flag:intro.soloJob
them: You did a job on your own! Your mum told the whole bus. Then the bus told me. Then I told the café.
them: Mrs Abbot made a cake. For you. We ate it. On your behalf. It was very good.
them: It had your name in icing, spelt a bit wrong. We ate that bit first. To hide the evidence.
them: The Bench raised their cups of tea to you. Someone made a speech. It was mostly about crisps.
them: Well done, idiot. Really. I'm proud of you. Don't let it go to your head.
--
when: cue:event.ionStorm.afterglow
them: A storm?! In space?! Your message said "it was loud". That's it? Loud? I need details, {me}!
them: Was there lightning? Space lightning? Did the lights flicker? Did you hide under a table? I would have.
them: You're fine, though. Good. That's the main thing.
them: Then I'm allowed to say: that's the coolest thing that's ever happened to anyone I know.
them: I told the Bench. I said "it was loud". They were very impressed. Bye!
--
when: came:any
them: You went to the {trad}! You! At a thing! Voluntarily! I'm telling the Bench. They won't believe it.
them: You once hid in the coats at a birthday party. For two hours. With a slice of cake. I found you.
them: And now look at you. Out there. With people. Going to things.
them: I hope there was food. There's always food at a good thing.
them: I'm really glad. You were always braver than you thought. Bye, {me}.
--
when: friend:any
them: {friend}, eh? Good. Tell {friend} they've got big shoes to fill. Bench-sized shoes.
them: I'm glad you've got someone up there. Honestly.
them: You were never any good at being on your own. You used to chat to the toaster.
them: Tell {friend} you snore. They should know. It's only fair.
them: Someone has to make sure you eat the crisps. Bye.   [laugh]
--
// W3, rec deck R12: Sam wants to know if you've beaten anyone at Lightdisc yet
them: {me}. Important question. Have you beaten anyone at Lightdisc yet? Yes or no. You can't answer. I'll decide.
them: I've decided no. You're trying very hard, though. I can tell. You've got a trying face.   [laugh]
them: I looked it up at the library. There's nothing. Lightdisc doesn't exist down here. You've got a secret sport.
them: I've made my own version. It's a frisbee and the café wall. Mrs Abbot has banned it.
them: Beat someone. Then tell me their name, so I can be smug on your behalf. Bye, idiot.
--
when: weekday:mon
them: Hi. Monday. Did you play Lightdisc at the weekend? You did. I bet you did. Did you win?
them: Don't tell me. If you won I'll be jealous, and if you lost I'll be mean about it. Both are fun.   [laugh]
them: I'm picturing you diving for a glowing disc. Very heroic. Slightly ridiculous. That's you, really.
them: Next call, tell me the score. Real score. Not the one you'd like. Bye, {me}.
